== Chapter II

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The principal assertion about the necessity of mortification set out to be confirmed — Mortification the duty of the best believers, Col 3:5; 1 Cor 9:27 — Indwelling sin always remains; no perfection in this life, Phil 3:12; 1 Cor 13:12; 2 Pet 3:18; Gal 5:17, etc. — The activity of remaining sin in believers, Rom 7:23; Jas 4:5; Heb 12:1 — Its fruitfulness and tendency — Every lust aims at the height of its kind — The Spirit and the new nature given to contend against indwelling sin, Gal 5:17; 2 Pet 1:4, 5; Rom 7:23 — The fearful outcome of neglecting mortification, Rev 3:2; Heb 3:13 — The first general principle of the whole discourse confirmed by this — The lack of this duty lamented.]

#smallcaps[Having] laid this foundation, a brief confirmation of the main conclusions mentioned above will lead me to what I chiefly intend:

I. #emph[That the choicest believers, who are certainly freed from the condemning power of sin, ought still to make it their business all their days to mortify the indwelling power of sin.]

So the apostle says, “Put to death, therefore, the components of your earthly nature” (Col 3:5). To whom is he speaking? To those who had been “raised with Christ” (verse 1); who had “died” with him (verse 3); whose life Christ was, and who would “appear with Him in glory” (verse 4). You#super[+] must mortify. Make it your#super[+] daily work. Be always at it while you#super[+] live. Do not stop for a day. Be killing sin, or it will be killing you#super[+]. Your#super[+] having died with Christ in principle, and been made alive with him, will not excuse you#super[+] from this work. And our Savior tells us how his Father deals with every branch in him that bears fruit, every true and living branch: “He prunes to make it even more fruitful” (John 15:2). He prunes it, and not for a day or two, but as long as it is a branch in this world. And the apostle tells you#super[+] what his own practice was: “I discipline my body and make it my slave” (1 Cor 9:27). “I do it daily,” he says. “It is the work of my life. I never leave it off; this is my business.” If this was the work and business of Paul, who was raised so incomparably far above the ordinary measure of believers in grace, revelations, experiences, privileges, and comforts, on what possible ground can we claim to be excused from this work and duty while we are in this world? A short account of the reasons for this may be given:

1\. Indwelling sin always #emph[remains] while we are in this world; so it must always be mortified. I will not deal now with the vain, foolish, and ignorant disputes of men about keeping the commands of God perfectly, about perfection in this life, and about being wholly and perfectly dead to sin. It is more than likely that the men who hold those abominations never knew what it is to keep any one of God’s commands. They are so far below perfection of degree that they have never reached perfection of parts, that is, obedience to the whole law, or universal obedience in sincerity. That is why many in our days who have talked of perfection have been wiser, and have claimed that it consists in knowing no difference between good and evil. Not that they are perfect in the things we call good, but that everything is alike to them, and the height of wickedness is their perfection. Others have found a new way to it, by denying original, indwelling sin, and by bringing down the spiritual demands of the law of God to suit men’s fleshly hearts. They have shown clearly enough that they do not know the life of Christ and its power in believers, and so they have invented a new righteousness that the gospel knows nothing of, puffed up without reason by their fleshly minds. As for us, who dare not be wise beyond what is written, nor boast through other men’s lives of what God has not done for us, we say that indwelling sin lives in us, in some measure and degree, while we are in this world. We dare not speak as if we “have already obtained all this, or have already been made perfect” (Phil 3:12). Our “inner self is being renewed day by day” as long as we live here (2 Cor 4:16), and as the new is renewed, so the breaches and decays of the old are repaired. While we are here we “know in part” (1 Cor 13:12). There is a darkness remaining in us, to be removed by degrees as we “grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Pet 3:18). And “the flesh craves what is contrary to the Spirit … so that you#super[+] do not do what you#super[+] want” (Gal 5:17). So we fall short in our obedience as well as in our light (1 John 1:8). We have a “body of death” (Rom 7:24), from which we are not delivered except by the death of our bodies (Phil 3:21). Now, since it is our duty to mortify, to be killing sin while it is in us, we must be at work. The man appointed to kill an enemy, if he stops striking before the other stops living, does only half his work (Gal 6:9; Heb 12:1; 2 Cor 7:1).

2\. Sin does not merely remain in us. It is still #emph[active,] still working to bring forth the deeds of the flesh. If sin left us alone, we might leave sin alone. But sin is never less quiet than when it seems most quiet, and its waters are usually deepest where they are still. So our plans against it must be vigorous at all times and in every condition, even where we least suspect it. Sin not only remains in us; there is “another law at work in my body, warring against the law of my mind” (Rom 7:23), and “the spirit that dwells in us yearns with envy” (Jas 4:5). It is always at work. “The flesh craves what is contrary to the Spirit” (Gal 5:17). Lust is forever tempting and conceiving sin (Jas 1:14). In every moral act it is doing one of three things: inclining us to evil, holding us back from good, or unsettling our spirit for communion with God. It inclines us to evil: “I keep on doing the evil I do not want to do,” says the apostle (Rom 7:19). Why? “Nothing good lives in me, that is, in my flesh.” It holds us back from good: “I do not do the good I want to do” (verse 19). That is, “For the same reason, either I do not do it, or I do not do it as I should; all my holy things are defiled by this sin.” “They oppose each other, so that you#super[+] do not do what you#super[+] want” (Gal 5:17). And it unsettles our spirit; that is why it is called “the sin that so easily entangles” (Heb 12:1), and why the apostle complains of it so bitterly in Romans 7. So sin is always acting, always conceiving, always seducing and tempting. Who can say he has ever had anything to do with God, or done anything for God, without indwelling sin having a hand in corrupting what he did? And it will keep up this trade, more or less, all our days. If sin is always acting, then unless we are always putting it to death, we are lost. The man who stands still and takes blow after blow without resisting will be conquered in the end. If sin is subtle, watchful, strong, and always at work killing our souls, while we are lazy, careless, and foolish about destroying it, can we expect things to end well? Not a day passes but sin either defeats us or is defeated, prevails or is prevailed against; and so it will be as long as we live in this world.

I will release from this duty the man who can bring sin to terms, to a truce in this war. If sin will spare him for one day, in one single duty (provided he understands how spiritual obedience is and how subtle sin is), let him say to his soul, as far as this duty goes, “Soul, take your rest.” The saints, whose souls long to be delivered from sin’s harassing rebellion, know there is no safety against it except constant warfare.

3\. Sin will not only be striving, acting, rebelling, troubling, and disturbing. If it is left alone, if it is not continually mortified, it will #emph[bring forth great, cursed, scandalous, soul-destroying sins]. The apostle tells us what its works and fruits are: “The acts of the flesh are obvious: sexual immorality, impurity, and debauchery; idolatry and sorcery; hatred, discord, jealousy, and rage; rivalries, divisions, factions, and envy; drunkenness, orgies, and the like” (Gal 5:19–21). You#super[+] know what it did in David and several others. Sin always aims at the utmost. Every time it rises up to tempt or entice, if it could have its own way, it would go on to the utmost sin of that kind. Every unclean thought or glance would be adultery if it could. Every covetous desire would be oppression, and every thought of unbelief would be atheism, if it were allowed to grow to its full head. Men may reach the point where sin is not heard speaking a scandalous word in their hearts, that is, urging them to some great sin with scandal in its mouth. Yet every rising of lust, if it had its way, would come to the height of wickedness: it is like the grave, which is never satisfied. And here lies no small part of the deceitfulness of sin, by which it succeeds in hardening men, and so in ruining them (Heb 3:13). It is modest, as it were, in its first stirrings and suggestions. But once it has gained a footing in the heart by them, it holds its ground firmly and presses on to further degrees of the same kind. This new activity and pressing forward makes the soul take little notice of how far it has already gone in falling away from God. It thinks all is fairly well if there is no further progress. And so far as the soul is made insensible of any sin, that is, without the sense of it the gospel requires, so far it is hardened. But sin keeps pressing forward, because it has no limit short of utterly forsaking God and opposing him. That it moves toward its height by degrees, holding the ground it has gained by hardness, comes not from its nature but from its deceitfulness. Now nothing can prevent this but mortification. That withers the root and strikes at the head of sin every hour, so that whatever it aims at, it is thwarted. There is not the best saint in the world who, if he gave up this duty, would not fall into as many cursed sins as anyone of his kind ever did.

4\. This is one main reason why the Spirit and the new nature are given to us: so that we may have a principle within us by which to oppose sin and lust. “The flesh craves what is contrary to the Spirit.” Well, and what then? Why, “and the Spirit what is contrary to the flesh” (Gal 5:17). There is an inclination in the Spirit, or the spiritual new nature, to act against the flesh, just as there is in the flesh to act against the Spirit. So also 2 Pet 1:4, 5: it is our sharing in the divine nature that lets us escape the corruption in the world caused by lust. And in Rom 7:23 there is a law of the mind as well as a law of the members. Now, when two fighters are engaged, it is, first, the most unjust and unreasonable thing in the world to tie one up and keep him from doing his utmost, and to leave the other free to wound him as he pleases. And, secondly, it is the most foolish thing in the world to tie up the one who fights for our eternal condition \[salvation?\] and to leave alone the one who seeks and violently attempts our everlasting ruin. The fight is for our lives and souls. Not to be daily using the Spirit and the new nature to mortify sin is to neglect the excellent help God has given us against our greatest enemy. If we neglect to make use of what we have received, God may justly hold back his hand from giving us more. His graces, as well as his gifts, are given to us to use, exercise, and trade with. Not to be daily mortifying sin is to sin against the goodness, kindness, wisdom, grace, and love of God, who has supplied us with a principle for doing it.

5\. Neglect of this duty throws the soul into a condition exactly opposite to the one the apostle says was his: “Though our outer self is wasting away, yet our inner self is being renewed day by day” (2 Cor 4:16). In such men the inner self wastes away, and the outer self is renewed day by day. Sin is like the house of David, and grace like the house of Saul. #emph[Exercise] and #emph[success] are the two main things that nourish grace in the heart. When grace is allowed to lie still, it withers and decays; the things of it are “about to die” (Rev 3:2), and sin gains ground toward hardening the heart (Heb 3:13). This is what I mean: when this duty is left undone, grace withers, lust flourishes, and the state of the heart grows worse and worse. And the Lord knows what desperate and fearful outcomes this has had with many. Where sin, through the neglect of mortification, wins a considerable victory, it breaks the bones of the soul (Ps 31:10; 51:8), and makes a man weak, sick, and ready to die (Ps 38:3–5), so that he cannot look up (Ps 40:12; Isa 33:24). And when poor creatures will take blow after blow, wound after wound, defeat after defeat, and never rouse themselves to vigorous resistance, can they expect anything but to be hardened through the deceitfulness of sin, and for their souls to bleed to death (2 John 8)? Indeed, it is a sad thing to consider the fearful outcomes of this neglect, which lie before our eyes every day. Do we not see those whom we knew as humble, soft-hearted, broken-hearted Christians, tender and afraid to offend, zealous for God and all his ways, his Sabbaths and ordinances, now grown earthly, carnal, cold, wrathful, and conformed to the men of the world and the things of the world, through neglecting to watch over this duty? They are a scandal to religion and a fearful temptation to those who know them. The truth is this. Between placing mortification in a rigid, stubborn frame of spirit, which is for the most part earthly, legal, censorious, and partial, and consistent with wrath, envy, malice, and pride, on the one hand, and claims of liberty, grace, and I know not what, on the other, true gospel mortification is almost lost among us. More of this later.

6\. It is our duty to be “perfecting holiness in the fear of God” (2 Cor 7:1); to “grow in the grace” every day (1 Pet 2:2; 2 Pet 3:18); to have our “inner self … renewed day by day” (2 Cor 4:16). Now, this cannot be done without mortifying sin daily. Sin sets its strength against every act of holiness, and against every degree of it we grow to. Let no man think he is making any progress in holiness who does not walk over the bellies of his lusts. The man who does not kill sin in his way takes no steps toward his journey’s end. The man who meets no opposition from it, and who does not set himself to mortify it in every particular, is at peace with it, not dying to it.

This, then, is the first general principle of the discourse that follows. Sin has been put to death, if I may put it so, by merit, all of it and every sin, in the cross of Christ. A real foundation of universal mortification has been laid in our first conversion, by conviction of sin, humiliation for sin, and the planting of a new principle that opposes sin and destroys it. And yet, in spite of all this, sin still so remains, so acts and works in the best of believers while they live in this world, that the constant, daily mortification of it is their duty all their days. Before I go on to consider the next principle, I cannot help complaining in passing about many who profess the faith in these days. Instead of bringing forth the great and evident fruits of mortification that are expected, they hardly bear any leaves of it. There is indeed a broad light that has fallen on the men of this generation, and with it many spiritual gifts have been given. These, with some other things, have wonderfully widened the bounds of those who profess and of profession itself; both have multiplied and increased greatly. So there is a noise of religion and religious duties in every corner, and preaching in abundance. And it is not empty, light, trivial, and vain, as it used to be, but has a good measure of spiritual gift. If you measure the number of believers by light, gifts, and profession, the church may well say, “Who has begotten these for me?” But if you measure them by this great distinguishing grace of Christians, perhaps you will find their number not so multiplied. Where can we find almost any one of those who owe their conversion to these days of light, who talk and profess at a level of spirituality that few in former days knew anything of (I will not judge them, but perhaps they are boasting of what the Lord has done in them), who does not give evidence of a miserably unmortified heart? If wasting time, idleness, unprofitableness in men’s callings, envy, strife, discord, rivalry, wrath, pride, worldliness, and selfishness (1 Cor 1) are the badges of Christians, we have them on us and among us in abundance. And if it is so with those who have much light, and light that we hope is saving, what shall we say of some who want to be counted religious, and yet despise gospel light? As for the duty we have in hand, they know nothing more of it than men denying themselves at times in outward comforts, which is one of its outermost branches, and which even so they seldom practice. May the good Lord send out a spirit of mortification to cure our disorders, or we are in a sad condition!

There are two evils that certainly attend every unmortified professing Christian: the first in himself, the second in respect of others.

1\. #emph[In himself.] Let him claim what he will, he has #emph[light thoughts of sin], at least of sins of daily weakness. The root of an unmortified life is swallowing sin without bitterness in the heart. When a man has trained his imagination to think of grace and mercy in such a way that he can swallow and digest daily sins without bitterness, that man is on the very brink of turning the grace of God into a license for sin, and of being hardened by the deceitfulness of sin. Nor is there any greater evidence in the world of a false and rotten heart than to carry on such a trade. To use the blood of Christ, which is given to #emph[cleanse] us (1 John 1:7; Titus 2:14); the exaltation of Christ, which is to give us #emph[repentance] (Acts 5:31); and the doctrine of grace, which teaches us to #emph[renounce ungodliness] (Titus 2:11, 12), to excuse sin, is a rebellion that in the end will break the bones. Through this door most of the professing Christians who have fallen away in our days have gone out from us. For a while most of them were under conviction. This kept them to their duties and brought them to profess the faith, so that they “escaped the corruption of the world through the knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Pet 2:20). But having become acquainted with the doctrine of the gospel, and being weary of duty, for which they had no principle within, they began to excuse themselves in many neglects on the strength of the doctrine of grace. Now, once this evil had taken hold of them, they quickly tumbled into perdition.

2\. #emph[To others.] It has an evil influence on them in two ways:

(1.) It #emph[hardens] them, by making them believe that they are in as good a condition as the best professing Christians. Whatever they see in such men is so stained by the lack of this mortification that it is worth nothing to them. These men have a zeal for religion, but it goes with a lack of patience and of universal righteousness. They give up extravagance, but for worldliness. They separate from the world, but live wholly for themselves, taking no care to show loving-kindness on the earth. Or they talk spiritually and live vainly; speak of communion with God and are in every way conformed to the world; boast of the forgiveness of sin and never forgive others. And with such thoughts poor creatures harden their hearts in their unregenerate state.

(2.) It #emph[deceives] them, by making them believe that if they can come up to the condition of such men, all will be well with them. So it becomes an easy thing to have the great temptation of a reputation in religion to wrestle with, when they may go far beyond such men in what appears outwardly, and yet fall short of eternal life. But of these things, and of all the evils of an unmortified walk, more later.
